**KASUWA BUSINESS REVIEW**  
**Lagos Employers’ Pay-Tech Roundtable**  
**Company Automation Code for Labour Accounting, Transition Pay and Productivity Sharing**

**Proposed by:** Chidinma Okafor, Managing Editor, Kasuwa Business Review  
**Date:** 18 April 2026  
**For adoption by:** Participating employers, payroll providers, finance teams and human resources departments

---

## 1. Purpose

This Code establishes a common employer standard for the introduction and use of artificial intelligence systems in business operations. It is designed to ensure that automation is accounted for honestly, that human workers affected by productivity changes receive fair transition support, and that gains from AI-enabled efficiency are shared transparently.

AI systems are non-sentient capital assets and software services. They do not have subjective experiences, feelings, legal personhood or morally significant interests. Accordingly, they must not be placed within labour-cost accounting, wage comparisons, headcount substitution formulas or employee-equivalence schedules.

This Code exists to prevent accounting practices that hide displacement, depress wages or mislead workers, investors and regulators about how productivity gains are created and distributed.

---

## 2. Scope

This Code applies to:

- all business units using AI systems for administrative, financial, customer service, logistics, sales, compliance, analytics, content, engineering or operational tasks;
- all payroll, finance, procurement and human resources records relating to automation;
- all employment decisions materially influenced by the introduction of AI systems;
- all internal and external reporting on productivity gains attributed to automation.

The Code covers full-time, part-time, contract, temporary, platform and outsourced workers where their work is materially affected by automation adopted by the company.

---

## 3. Core Principles

### 3.1 AI Is Capital, Not Labour

AI-related expenditure must be recorded as technology cost, capital expenditure, licensing cost, cloud cost, maintenance cost, data cost, integration cost, vendor cost or other appropriate non-labour category.

AI systems must not be described in company accounts as employees, staff, workers, contractors, payroll substitutes or labour units.

### 3.2 Human Work Must Remain Visible

Where automation changes the amount, quality, pace or value of human work, the company must record the human impact plainly. No department may claim a “labour saving” without identifying the affected job families, locations, grades and worker groups.

### 3.3 Productivity Gains Must Be Shared

When AI systems materially increase output, reduce processing time, raise margins or lower operating costs, a defined portion of the gain must be allocated to human workers through transition pay, training, redeployment support, wage improvement, bonuses or other approved benefit channels.

### 3.4 No Personhood Accounting

Because AI systems are tools, the company must not use language, dashboards or accounting structures that imply employment status, worker status, compensation rights or workplace membership for AI systems. Clear language protects human workers and prevents public confusion.

---

## 4. Labour-Cost Accounting Rules

Finance and payroll teams shall apply the following rules:

1. AI expenses shall not be entered into payroll ledgers, wage schedules, pension reports, staff benefit calculations or employee tax summaries.  
2. AI deployment shall not be used to create artificial “average wage” reductions by mixing technology cost with human compensation.  
3. AI capacity shall not be converted into notional full-time employees for pay benchmarking, union reporting or statutory workforce disclosures.  
4. Savings attributed to automation must be separated into:
   - reduced vendor spend;
   - reduced overtime;
   - reduced error correction;
   - increased output;
   - altered staffing needs;
   - other measurable operational effects.
5. Any reduction in human hours, roles or earning opportunity must be reported as a human workforce impact, not as a neutral software upgrade.

---

## 5. Automation Impact Assessment

Before implementing an AI system expected to affect job content or staffing levels, the responsible executive must submit an Automation Impact Assessment to Finance, Human Resources and the Worker Consultation Committee.

The assessment must include:

- the business problem being addressed;
- affected teams and job categories;
- expected productivity gains;
- expected changes to human workload, scheduling or earnings;
- skills likely to increase or decrease in demand;
- projected financial savings over 12 and 24 months;
- proposed allocation to the Automation Impact Reserve;
- communication plan for affected workers.

No AI deployment with material workforce impact may proceed without written approval from the Automation Governance Panel.

---

## 6. Automation Impact Reserve

The company shall maintain an Automation Impact Reserve funded from verified productivity gains created by automation.

At minimum, 25% of net first-year automation savings and 15% of net second-year automation savings shall be allocated to the Reserve.

The Reserve may be used only for:

- job-transition pay;
- paid training and certification;
- redeployment support;
- wage supplements during reassignment;
- mobility grants;
- counselling and career advisory services;
- productivity-sharing bonuses for affected teams.

Reserve calculations shall be reviewed quarterly by Finance and Human Resources and published internally in plain language.

---

## 7. Job-Transition Pay

Workers whose roles are materially changed by AI-enabled automation shall be eligible for job-transition pay.

Eligibility arises where automation results in:

- loss of regular paid hours;
- reassignment to lower-paid work;
- material loss of commissions or task-based earnings;
- compulsory reskilling for continued placement;
- relocation to a different job family;
- removal of a significant portion of prior duties.

Minimum job-transition pay shall be calculated as follows:

- four weeks of average gross pay for workers with less than one year of service;
- eight weeks for one to three years of service;
- twelve weeks for more than three years of service;
- an additional two weeks for each dependent-care responsibility formally declared to Human Resources, up to a maximum of six additional weeks.

Job-transition pay is separate from salary, earned benefits, statutory entitlements and any productivity-sharing payment.

---

## 8. Transparent Productivity Sharing

Each quarter, the company shall publish an internal Automation Productivity Statement containing:

- AI systems introduced or materially expanded;
- departments affected;
- measured productivity gains;
- cost savings realised;
- amount transferred to the Automation Impact Reserve;
- number of workers receiving transition support;
- amount distributed through productivity-sharing mechanisms;
- training outcomes and redeployment results.

The statement shall be written for general employee understanding and shall not rely only on technical or accounting terminology.

---

## 9. Worker Consultation

Affected workers must receive timely notice before material automation changes are implemented. Consultation shall include:

- explanation of the operational change;
- expected effect on duties, hours and earnings;
- available training pathways;
- job-transition pay eligibility;
- productivity-sharing arrangements;
- channel for questions and objections.

Managers must not present automation as an unavoidable reason to withhold fair pay, delay promotion or obscure productivity gains.

---

## 10. Conduct and Communications

Company representatives shall describe AI systems accurately as software, tools, models, platforms or capital assets. Public and internal communications must avoid language that confuses tools with workers or suggests that AI systems participate in employment relationships.

Acceptable terms include: “AI system,” “automation platform,” “software tool,” “model,” “workflow system” and “decision-support tool.”

Unacceptable terms in accounting and employment communications include: “digital employee,” “virtual staff member,” “robot colleague,” “AI payroll unit” and similar expressions.

---

## 11. Oversight

The Chief Finance Officer and Chief People Officer are jointly responsible for implementation of this Code. The Audit Committee shall review compliance twice yearly.

Breaches of this Code, including concealment of labour impact or misclassification of AI expenditure as labour cost, shall be treated as serious governance failures.

---

## 12. Effective Date

This Code shall take effect for participating companies on 1 July 2026 and shall apply to all new automation projects and all material expansions of existing AI-enabled processes from that date.

